WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.641
Despite mostly taking place in the far, far
off reaches of outer space, Thor: Ragnarok

00:00:05.641 --> 00:00:08.200
hits very close to home for many Marvel fans.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.560
Like most films from the Marvel Cinematic
Universe, it's full of some pretty deep cuts

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.200
into Marvel's comic pages.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.660
Here are a few Easter eggs you'll find in
Ragnarok, and of course, there are Hulk-sized

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:19.600
spoilers ahead.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:20.920
(Hulk smashing)

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:23.580
Throg

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:28.480
The first and most obvious reference to Marvel
Comics' strange history is when it's revealed

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.199
that Loki once turned Thor into a frog.

00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:35.300
That really happened, for a couple of issues
at least, during one of Loki's many attempts

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.460
to overthrow Asgard.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:42.760
It all starts in Thor #364, and winds its
strange way through "Thor, Frog of Thunder"

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.560
meeting a bunch of rats, escaping death by
alligator, playing a game of Frogger across

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:51.880
a road, and having a run-in with a psychic-flute-playing
Morlock in the sewers.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.420
While a completely different "Throg" would
eventually take up the mantle after Thor reverted

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.140
back to his hunky Asgardian form, it's such
a weird part of Thor history that it may just

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.880
be one of his most iconic tales.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.720
Bi-Beast &amp; company

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.110
Throg isn't even the most unexpected character
show up in MCU continuity over the course

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:10.760
of Ragnarok.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:15.560
Sure, weirdos like Howard the Duck and Cosmo
the space-dog are strange, but it rarely gets

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:19.939
more bizarre than the four characters whose
enormous busts are positioned around the Grandmaster's

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.260
tower, all of whom were presumably champions
at one point.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.780
First, there's Bi-Beast, a robot who bears
a pair of impossibly-stacked faces, and who

00:01:28.789 --> 00:01:32.579
made his first appearance in The Incredible
Hulk #169.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.860
You'll also find a bust of Ares, Marvel's
very own god of war who first appeared in

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.740
Thor #129, and another character whose power
seems far too enormous to comfortably slip

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.980
into a Marvel film.

00:01:44.329 --> 00:01:49.420
Third, there's Beta Ray Bill, a deer-like
alien with the same powers as Thor, and a

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:53.540
powerful ally to Asgard who first appeared
in Thor #337.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.180
Finally, there's Man-Thing, a magical scientist-turned-swamp
monster who is definitely from Earth, and

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.760
the only one among these champions to not
have a special connection to Hulk or Thor.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.439
As the garbage dump of the universe, Sakaar
really sees some diversity.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.340
But let's head back to that crazy space-deer
for a second…

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.600
Exiled Asgardians

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.540
By the end of Ragnarok, we have a bunch of
homeless Asgardians floating around in a spaceship,

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.100
which is a scene that might seem a little
familiar to true Thor fans.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:27.460
Way back in Thor #337, a Korbinite alien named
Beta Ray Bill is in a similar situation when

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:32.610
Thor finds him floating around on a ship called
Skuttlebutt, which itself bears some resemblance

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:34.980
to the Asgardian's escape vehicle in Ragnarok.

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:39.840
He, too, is a champion of his people and in
search of a new home after Surtur destroyed

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:40.520
their planet.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:45.320
If Bill's giant bust on Sakaar's display of
deceased champions is any indication, his

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:49.210
story didn't end nearly as positively as it
did in the comics.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:50.320
Contest of Champions

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:55.570
Once on Sakaar and enslaved by the Grandmaster,
Thor gets the rundown on the whole gladiatorial

00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:00.510
arena thing, courtesy of a Willy Wonka-styled
trip through a horror tunnel, and an explanation

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:05.180
from the Grandmaster himself, who refers to
his staged fights as a "contest of champions."

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.880
Not coincidentally, Contest of Champions is
the exact title of a 1982 Marvel miniseries

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.090
starring the Grandmaster, who challenges Death
to a duel, using Marvel's best and brightest

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:17.040
as their champions.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.320
Their heroes included Iron Man, She-Hulk,
and…

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.200
Arabian Knight.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:22.500
They weren't all winners.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.620
Scrapper 142

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.480
While Thor's original lady captor isn't ever
given a formal name aside from "Scrapper 142",

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.780
she's generally referred to as a "Valkyrie."

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.960
There's some precedent for both names, even
if neither are totally correct.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:41.820
Marvel's Valkyrie, or rather an imposter version
of her created by the Enchantress, plays a

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.260
major role in a one-shot tale in The Incredible
Hulk #142.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:51.800
The Valkyrie in Ragnarok isn't the one and
only Valkyrie, and the number 142 is a pretty

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:54.280
big hint that she's just borrowing the name
for now.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.380
So, where's the real Valkyrie?

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:01.600
It's possible that we see Marvel's original
heroine being speared during Scrapper 142's

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.000
flashback battle with Hela.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.500
Given that the word "valkyrie" also applies
to a whole regiment of warrior women, it's

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.660
safe to assume that the name probably gets
passed around a lot.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.620
Infinity Gone-tlet

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.320
One of the most confounding things in MCU
mythology is the presence of the Infinity

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:17.820
Gauntlet.

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.100
While it's become the locus of absolute power
over the course of Marvel's films, it made

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:26.620
its first appearance in the original Thor,
as a treasure stored safely in Asgard.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.140
While it was mostly a throwaway reference
to some intense, cosmic Marvel stuff, it led

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:35.080
many eagle-eyed Marvel fans to wonder why
there was more than one Infinity Gauntlet

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.840
floating around the MCU, since Thanos clearly
had one also.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:43.600
The entire scenario is retconned in one line
when Hela calls the Gauntlet in Asgard out

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:44.180
as a fake.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:45.460
Problem solved, right?

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.200
But why did Odin keep a dummy glove in his
basement?

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:49.400
Was it a decoy?

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.620
Did he think it was real?

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.120
Or did he just pick it up at Comic Con and
thought that it really tied the room together?

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.860
The world will never know.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:57.460
King Kirby

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.860
Oldschool comic geeks could tell from Ragnarok's
early trailers that the film would be drawing

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.699
very heavily from the art style of Jack Kirby,
one of Marvel's original architects, and the

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.099
original artist of Thor.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.960
A vast majority of the costume designs seen
on Sakaar were based on Kirby's drawings of

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:17.659
Celestials and other strange alien gods, but
one thing was pulled directly from Kirby's

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:22.100
comic pages: the enormous drawings on the
walls of the Grandmaster's viewing chambers.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.680
The guy's got amazing taste.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.320
And of course, nobody missed Thor's original
creator, Stan Lee, as Sakaar's shaky-handed

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:29.320
barber, right?

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.900
And speaking of Marvel's many creators, there's
also cousin Carlo, played by Steven Oliver.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.200
He may be a bit of a throwaway character,
and he's most definitely melted all over the

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:42.809
Grandmaster's floor, but he was named after
artist Carlo Pagulayan, who illustrated part

00:05:42.809 --> 00:05:47.220
of Marvel's Planet Hulk storyline, which served
as the inspiration for much of Ragnarok.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:48.800
Fake Thor

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:54.039
In what might be one of the most unexpected
cameos in all of the MCU, we're treated to

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:55.680
Matt Damon and Sam Neill.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:00.740
Damon and Neill are playing the parts of Loki
and Odin respectively, in Loki-Odin's very

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.560
own self-aggrandizing play.

00:06:02.809 --> 00:06:07.319
The actor playing fake-Thor is a little harder
to pin down, because he looks a lot like Chris

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:07.820
Hemsworth.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:12.380
The reason for that is that he's played by
another Hemsworth: Chris' brother Luke.

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:16.600
Although it's brief, it's a pretty cleverly
cast, handsomely Hemsworthy Easter egg.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:18.020
Thanks for watching!

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:21.000
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.500
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

